Least-Privilege Capability Envelopes (REALM Integration)

To restrict an agent's operational scope, Phase 003H integrates the REALM framework for minimal task asset allocation. REALM constructs task-specific capability envelopes using precise dependency graphs and highly restricted API scopes.

Rather than granting broad ambient authority, REALM isolates the exact files, databases, and operational endpoints required for a validated task sub-node. In empirical evaluations, restricting asset availability via dependency graphs reduced task attack success rates from 16.6% down to 0.2%.

The capability subsystem operates under a mandatory operational invariant:

\text{Asset Discovery} \neq \text{Execution Authority}

An agent discovering a file, system resource, or network endpoint during task execution receives zero implicit authority to access or execute operations against that resource. Any newly discovered asset requires an explicit, out-of-band capability grant issued by the system policy engine.

Long-Context Evidence Partitioning (DISCO Integration)

To allow agents to ground actions in massive context stores without granting canonical memory-mutation authority, Phase 003H incorporates the DISCO (Distributed Long Context Scaling) framework. DISCO separates context retrieval and evidence grounding from canonical state reasoning. Worker models independently process context partitions to extract target evidence, achieving 78.4% accuracy on million-token RULER-QA benchmarks while reducing inference costs by over 80%. This evidence is presented to the memory kernel as unverified context, preventing unvalidated long-context reads from corrupting long-term state.

