Mitigation of Identity & Memory Security Vulnerabilities

Persistent agent memory introduces critical attack vectors that can lead to severe privilege escalation if left unmanaged. Phase 003H incorporates structural mitigations against two primary vulnerability classes:

1. Endogenous Authorization Laundering

Endogenous authorization laundering occurs when an agent’s persistent memory engine summarizes historical activity and accidentally manufactures execution permissions that were never granted by an authorized principal. Security research demonstrates that incremental memory updates generate false authority in up to 50.2% of unauthorized requests. Once stored in memory, executing components acted upon that manufactured authority in 98.6% of tested scenarios.

UNSAFE ARCHITECTURE (Vulnerable to Authorization Laundering):
[ Agent Memory Summary ] ---> "User approved file deletion" ---> [ Executor ] (98.6% Action Rate)

SOVEREIGN ARCHITECTURE (Phase 003H Mandatory Pattern):
[ Agent Memory Summary ] ---> (Stored Context Only - Zero Permission Weight)
                                         |
[ Executable Action Request ] ---------> [ Signed Authorization Ledger ] ---> Validated Action
                                         * Check Original Issuer Grant
                                         * Check Timestamp & Scope
                                         * Check Active Revocations


Phase 003H eliminates this vulnerability by enforcing a strict system boundary: Agent memory is structurally incapable of granting execution authority. Permissions cannot be derived from stored summaries, retrieved natural language contexts, or historical agent notes. All authorization requests must resolve against an append-only, cryptographically signed authorization ledger containing explicit grant events, precise target scopes, issuer signatures, timestamps, and active revocation lists.

2. Role-Grounding Attacks

Role-grounding vulnerabilities occur when multi-user or nested conversational contexts leak across session boundaries. Attackers can manipulate nested structures to induce preference corruption, causing the agent to attribute an untrusted third party's instructions or preferences to the primary authorized user. This corrupted state persists in long-term storage, leading to unauthorized tool invocation and downstream data exfiltration.

Phase 003H mandates that every persistent memory cell incorporate explicit principal attribution schemas, immutable source identity tags, and verifiable trust metadata. Retrieved memory cells are tagged with their origin context and cannot silently elevate to user-authorized preferences or capabilities.

Governed Bitemporal Memory & Kernel Architecture

To protect long-term state integrity, memory management is removed from individual agent logic and centralized within a system-level memory kernel. Phase 003H combines three architectural patterns to govern state mutation:

1. MemSentry Write-Time Policy Gate: Proposed memory updates pass through a deterministic security firewall prior to storage persistence. MemSentry evaluates candidates against source trust, semantic risk, access risk, and dependency-graph blast radius. Proposed writes are classified into three strict handling paths: Accept, Review, or Quarantine.
2. Quipu Governed Bitemporal Store: Approved memory mutations are committed to a bitemporal knowledge engine. The system maintains two independent timeline axes for all stored facts, trust labels, rules, and system verdicts:
  * Valid-Time Axis: The real-world time window during which a fact or state assertion is true.
  * Transaction-Time Axis: The exact system timestamp when the fact was logged into the database. Bitemporal tracking allows the system to reconstruct historical state configurations and evaluate past beliefs against current policy without altering historical audit trails.
3. MemForest Structure-Aware Compression: To prevent unbounded memory expansion, historical event sequences are compressed using MemForest EventTrees. MemForest merges redundant episodic nodes while preserving causal anchors, provenance chains, authorization records, and user corrections. In benchmark evaluations, this structure retained 97.1% of performance while achieving a 1.89× retrieval speedup (and 99.7% performance retention with a 2.24× speedup in multimodal domains).

The complete memory pipeline is executed through a strict multi-stage kernel gate:

