Task-Graph Contextual Integrity

Validating individual tool calls in isolation fails to prevent multi-step privacy breaches. Information that is safe to disclose to a primary sub-agent may violate system policy when combined with other context or forwarded downstream. Phase 004B enforces Task-Graph Contextual Integrity (utilizing the MAPLE-Bench evaluation methodology) to track information flow across entire multi-agent interaction graphs.

In experimental implementations across multi-agent workflows, Task-Graph Contextual Integrity reduced unauthorized downstream disclosures by 85.8% to 94.1% relative to local history baselines. Capability envelopes generated in Phase 004B explicitly bound downstream propagation scope, defining authorized recipients and downstream data limits alongside initial resource read rights.

Portable Provenance & Signed Resumable Task-State Objects

System artifacts—including generated code, parsed documents, API payloads, and execution outputs—must carry embedded lineage. Phase 004B incorporates the TRACEs framework to embed fine-grained execution context and data lineage directly into generated artifacts. Embedded lineage ensures provenance remains fully portable with the artifact rather than depending on external orchestrator logs.

State transitions emit two standardized cryptographic structures:

1. EvidenceBundle: Contains raw execution context, input lineage, environmental parameters, tool responses, and verifier evaluations.
2. CommitReceipt: A cryptographically signed record emitted by the Commit Plane confirming that an EvidenceBundle satisfied system policy and was committed to state.

To guarantee state continuity across process terminations, model substitutions, and node failures, the sovereign Commit Plane standardizes network state transitions around Signed Resumable Task-State Objects. Rather than transmitting arbitrary conversation logs, the unit of execution committed to the network is a versioned state container defined as:

\text{TaskState} = \{\text{AgentDID}, \text{StateVersion}, \text{MemoryRootHash}, \text{SkillManifest}, \text{AuthScope}, \text{VerificationState}\}

Phase 004B enforces the Pufibara state-binding rule to prevent stale or detached evidence from authorizing state mutations:

\text{artifact\_hash} \longrightarrow \text{execution} \longrightarrow \text{evidence} \longrightarrow \text{verdict}

All simulation, compilation, and policy evidence must bind directly to the exact cryptographic hash of the candidate artifact that produced it. Evidence generated against Version N of an artifact is structurally forbidden from authorizing the execution or deployment of Version N+1.

Append-Only Shared Evidence Ledgers (Agora Model)

For multi-agent collectives and decentralized coordination, Phase 004B implements an append-only, Git-based Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) shared memory ledger based on the Agora model. Decoupled agents log claims, hypotheses, subtask parents, evidence bundles, and verification statuses as immutable, content-addressed Git commits (demonstrated in 12-day autonomous agent collective runs yielding 1,703 contributions and 165 independent reproductions without failure).

This DAG architecture allows decentralized workers to propose branch hypotheses and share verified execution results without relying on a central database. A worker node can disconnect from the mesh while leaving its contributions independently verifiable, inspectable, and reproducible by any peer on the network.

These verifiable commit receipts and portable provenance structures provide the prerequisite security primitives for expanding execution across untrusted nodes in Phase 004C.

