Control-Plane-Free Mesh Topology & Routing

Phase 004C replaces centralized orchestrators with Cascadia control-plane-free mesh topologies. Nodes communicate over libp2p and QUIC protocols, discovering capabilities through signed gossip advertisements and exchanging loads directly via peer-to-peer mechanisms. In experimental benchmarks, three-node Cascadia configurations achieved a 3.10× response throughput increase over single-node baselines under concurrent workloads.

Governance Limitation Warning: While Cascadia achieves control-plane-free inference routing, node admission still depends on an operator-managed Certificate Authority (CA). Decentralized peer compute execution does not automatically confer self-sovereign governance; authority gates must remain cryptographically enforced at the Commit Plane.

To route requests efficiently, the architecture integrates PlanetServe overlay routing principles. Peer routing dynamically adapts based on continuous network dimensions:

* Geographic locality and network latency
* Available link bandwidth
* Model fragment placement across nodes
* Dynamic hardware failure domains

Multi-agent communication graphs adopt Codebook Agent principles. Instead of broadcasting requests across fully connected, resource-intensive swarms, nodes select compact, task-specific communication graphs from a compressed topology codebook. This reduces network token consumption by 21.9% to 33.2% while selecting high-efficiency execution paths in ~2.4 milliseconds.

Communication-Efficient Speculative Decoding & Cache Reuse

Transmitting raw intermediate tensors or full probability distributions across Wide Area Networks (WANs) saturates connection bandwidth. Phase 004C deploys the DISCO (Distributed Speculative Decoding) framework for hybrid local/remote inference. DISCO replicates lightweight draft models across peer nodes and exchanges only token indices and scalar probabilities across WAN links. This preserves target distribution output while drastically cutting network communication overhead.

To prevent network link degradation from causing total execution stalls, nodes maintain AceSpec local probabilistic state caches. When a remote draft token candidate is rejected, AceSpec resolves continuation state locally, preventing WAN-wide pipeline rollbacks down to bandwidth limits of 50 Kbps.

The orchestrator determines state transport via explicit bandwidth decision thresholds:

\text{Decision} = \begin{cases} \text{Transfer KV State}, & \text{if Bandwidth} \ge \text{Threshold} \\ \text{Recompute State Locally}, & \text{if Bandwidth} < \text{Threshold} \end{cases}

For a 70B parameter Multi-Head Attention (MHA) model, the state transfer crossover threshold is 74–111 Gbps per stream. For Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) architectures, the threshold drops to 9–14 Gbps. If available bandwidth falls below these limits, the system automatically rebuilds state locally to minimize network latency.

At the node storage layer, high-throughput KV-cache networking utilizes FlatCake flat host storage configurations, binding host RAM and NVMe drives into a unified address space to sustain a 94% cache hit rate under heavy workloads (delivering 1.6× throughput over Mooncake baselines). Inter-node high-speed state transport utilizes Mooncake UCL-MPComm multi-rail RDMA networks for direct DRAM/VRAM state migration.

Malicious Node Defense & Economic Coordination

Untrusted peer nodes may return corrupted inference, altered weights, or malicious outputs. Phase 004C deploys canary-based integrity verification to inspect peer performance without performing full target model re-computation.

The verifying node periodically inserts secret canary inputs into subtask execution streams. By measuring abnormal activation drift against expected baselines, the system detects subverted or misconfigured model shards. Across 408 test configurations, canary drift measurement achieved 100% ranking accuracy in isolating malicious nodes from benign execution hardware.

Peer compute allocation and subtask distribution utilize sequential task auction protocols. Autonomous edge providers bid on subtasks based on available VRAM, thermal limits, model placement, and compute pricing. Auction markets maintain provider incentives under imperfect competition without granting external nodes access to sovereign system state.

Peer Networking, NAT Traversal & Single-Node Layer Splitting

Mesh transport standardizes on SwarmLLM peer-to-peer networking patterns. Communication operates under a strict operational stack:

1. End-to-end encrypted peer traffic using mTLS node authentication.
2. Local peer discovery via mDNS.
