Operating Principles Checklist

Engineering teams deploying Guardian Oracle or Q-Mesh components must verify compliance against this final operational checklist prior to production deployment:

* [ ] Isolation of Intent: Language models are strictly restricted to proposing candidate actions (CandidateResult). External policy engines and sovereign Commit Planes validate provenance and grant execution authority independently of model outputs.
* [ ] Memory Containment: Persistent memory serves exclusively as an auditable record of history and evidence. Memory cells are structurally prohibited from manufacturing permissions, overriding active policy ledgers, or granting execution capabilities.
* [ ] Substrate Independence: Core agent continuity—comprising W3C AgentDID identity, signed capability grants, durable memory roots, signed resumable TaskState objects, and versioned skill manifests—must survive process termination, model substitution, harness changes, and host machine failure.
* [ ] Granular Verification: Every state commit, document modification, or infrastructure change must cryptographically bind execution evidence directly to the exact artifact content hash that produced it:

\text{artifact\_hash} \longrightarrow \text{execution} \longrightarrow \text{evidence} \longrightarrow \text{verdict}

* [ ] Fail-Closed Release Semantics: Memory subsystems, verification gates, and release controllers must default to fail-closed state release whenever provenance chains, active revocation statuses, or valid-time parameters cannot be cryptographically verified.
* [ ] Zero Implicit Discovery Privilege: Discovering a system asset, database endpoint, or file resource during execution grants zero implicit authority to access or execute operations against that asset. All access requires explicit, out-of-band capability grants.
* [ ] Perceptual Data Isolation: All multimodal perceptual inputs (images, audio streams, raw files) are treated as untrusted evidence. Perception must be parsed into structured, validated observations before entering the planning module.
* [ ] Network Substrate Decoupling: Mesh applications operate independently of underlying peer transport mechanisms, executing identically across direct local connections, NVIDIA PAIR local subagent routing, direct NAT traversal paths, or authenticated fallback relays.
